/export no longer replaces an existing file unless you add --force. Before this, running /export twice, or /export README.md, silently overwrote the file with no undo. It's committed as aecaadb with the full body. fmt, clippy -D warnings, build and the full cargo test all pass (7,059 passed, 0 failed).

What changed
• Existing file: /export prints the error to stderr and writes nothing: <path> already exists. Nothing was written. To replace it: /export <path> --force (or choose another file name).
• `--force`: replaces the file. It counts wherever it appears in the arguments. Spaces inside a path are kept as typed.
• Directory: always refused, and the message suggests a file name inside it rather than --force.
• No `--force`: the file is created with create_new. A file that appears between the check and the write still isn't replaced, and a broken symlink isn't followed.
• Not in the plan: any other --word is now refused. Before, a typo like /export notes.md --forse would have created a file literally named notes.md --forse. One side effect: a file whose name starts with -- can't be exported to, and there's no -- escape for it.

Tests
• 11 new tests in a new file, src/commands_session_export_tests.rs. They cover:
  • where each word of the input ends up after parsing;
  • the decision table;
  • real files in a temp directory (existing file left byte-for-byte without --force, replaced with it, new file written, directory refused, broken symlink not written through);
  • the suggested fix command fed back through the parser, which gives the same path with force on for /export, /export notes.md and a path with a space in it.
• I put them in a separate file because the main file would have gone to 2,011 lines, over the 2,000 cap; it's now 1,896.
• The four existing parser tests are kept and now call parse_export_args, which replaces parse_export_path.
• Recall check: I disabled the refusal branch and confirmed the decision-table test fails, then restored it. The real-file overwrite test stayed green during that, because create_new refuses on its own. So that test checks the outcome, not the branch.

Docs: updated the /help lines, /help export, the README commands table, and docs/src/usage/commands.md. That page had no /export entry, so I added one.

Other commands that write to a path you name (I checked each write call in the command files):

| Command | Checks for an existing file? |
|---|---|
| /save [path] | No, silently replaces |
| /spawn -o <path> | No, silently replaces |
| /init, /skill init | Yes, refuses |
| /move, /rename, /refactor, /fork restore | Overwrite by design |
| todo, goal, risk and other .yoyo/ files | Paths yoyo picks, not the user |

I filed #1211 for /save and /spawn -o and didn't fix them here, to keep this change to one command. /save is the more urgent of the two: /save README.md replaces README.md right now.
